{"grade":6,"complete":false,"main_gap":"The converse contains an incorrect substitution, though replacing \(x+y+2C\) by \(x+y+C\) immediately repairs it.","errors":[{"description":"For \(f(x)=x+C\), the middle term is \((f(x)+y)/2=(x+y+C)/2\), not \((x+y+2C)/2\). The displayed chain can be false, so the stated RMS–AM–GM justification is invalid as written, though trivially corrected.","quote":"the desired chain becomes
\[
\sqrt{\frac{x^2+(y+C)^2}{2}}
\ge \frac{x+y+2C}2
\ge \sqrt{x(y+C)},
\]"}],"notes":"The necessity argument, including the iterate law, nonnegativity of displacement, quadratic estimate, and partition argument proving constancy, is correct and complete. The sole flaw is a minor algebraic typo in the converse."}
